Add a fluentd path for JSON formatted OpenStack logs.

oslo.log can emit JSON instead of its text format, which keeps fields
such as the request context structured rather than packed into a
message string that a regular expression then has to pick apart again.
Fluentd has had a JSON parser since Kerbside's logs needed one, and
etcd reuses it, but nothing lets an OpenStack service use it.

Add openstack_logging_json, defaulting to False. When it is set, the
global input parses JSON and takes the event time from the created
member, which is a float with full sub-second resolution -- asctime is
formatted with log_date_format and has none. A record transformer then
renames oslo.log's members to the names the text parser produces, so
the rest of the pipeline and anything reading the log store sees the
same fields either way, and lifts the request context fields back to
the top level. Everything else oslo.log emits is left in place, which
is the point of logging JSON. The context is kept whole because
oslo.context redacts auth_token before the formatter sees it.

This is one switch for every oslo.log service rather than a per service
option, because all of them share the single input in
conf/input/00-global.conf.j2. That input has one parser, so a mix of
formats underneath it cannot be read.

No service emits JSON yet, so this changes nothing until one is
converted. With the option off the rendered configuration is unchanged
apart from blank lines, which this template already produces from its
existing conditional.

Prompt: Michael asked how much work it would be to let every oslo.log
service in Kolla-Ansible log JSON using the parser added for Kerbside,
and asked for the structural changes to land in small patches ahead of
the bulk conversion. This is the second of those.

